 ## Proof
 
-1.1 Set $s_B=S(\mathcal N(\tau))$, $s_E=S(\mathcal N^c(\tau))$. Choose $\eta>0$ with $R<s_B-s_E-2\eta$. On $n$ uses, let $|v_n\rangle=(I\otimes V^{\otimes n})|\Phi_{d^n}\rangle$ in factor order $A'B^nE^n$. Let $P_n,Q_n$ be the spectral projectors for the IID receiver and environment states from [[lem-it-exponential-cq-spectral-projectors]], and let $\epsilon_n\le Ce^{-cn}$ bound the sum of their failure probabilities. The commuting joint projection has acceptance at least $1-\epsilon_n$. For $$|\widehat v_n\rangle=(I_{A'}\otimes P_n\otimes Q_n)|v_n\rangle,$$ the rank-two pure-vector calculation used in gentle measurement gives $$\bigl\||v_n\rangle\langle v_n|-|\widehat v_n\rangle\langle\widehat v_n|\bigr\|_1\le2\sqrt{\epsilon_n}.$$ This follows also by applying the gentle-measurement lemma to the pure state and the joint projection. Let $\omega=\operatorname{Tr}_{B^n}|\widehat v_n\rangle\langle\widehat v_n|$. Its environment rank is at most $e^{n(s_E+\eta)}$. Since the projected vector is pure, $$\operatorname{tr}(\omega^2)=\operatorname{tr}(\omega_{B^n}^2).$$ Moreover $$0\preceq\omega_{B^n}\preceq P_n\mathcal N(\tau)^{\otimes n}P_n\preceq e^{-n(s_B-\eta)}P_n.$$ To verify the first inequality, take the partial trace after $Q_n$; the complementary environment projection produces another positive summand of the original receiver marginal. Hence $\operatorname{tr}(\omega^2)\le e^{-n(s_B-\eta)}$, because its trace is at most one. [F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7, F8, F9, F10, given, algebra, construct]
